   coverage above is against the whole 33-CV corpus, so a word listed here is one no CV in the study contains - a keyword scorer can only ever call those missing, and that is the ceiling on this method, not a bug in it.

6) reading: rho 0.306 (retrieval-only) -> 0.787 (keyword).
   Three things this does NOT say:
   - it is not a RAG result. Coverage reads the whole document, so nothing here
     depends on retrieval; it prices the *scorer*, not the framework.
   - the corpus is synthetic and generate_dataset.py wrote the rubric's skill
     bullets using the requisition's own vocabulary. A keyword scorer is graded on
     the very tokens the generator was told to place, so this rho is the most
     generous measurement that method can ever get; on real CVs, which paraphrase,
     it will be lower. It is also gameable by keyword stuffing, which is why the
     study's headline stays the Q4_K_M row that reads the evidence.
   - the scale is compressed (see section 2): ranking improved, absolute scores did
     not track the labels, and mae is quoted next to rho for exactly that reason.
